{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch14t9ek68hLonQzBcGSK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The pieces fit together. In the last layer, 7 of 12 heads at [CLS] put 78–96% of their attention on the **question** tokens (heads 1, 3, 4, 7, 8, 9, 10). The final score is mostly a read-out of how well each question token was matched in the paragraph: lexical coverage. A bridge paragraph matches only its own hop of the question, so its coverage, and its score, is low.\n\nThe split-A feature diagnostics (not written into weights) point the same way. Up-weighting title match or down-weighting overlap helps a little. A paragraph-length penalty helps much more (tok90 945 → 747). That is plausible as mechanism, not just artifact: lexical-match evidence that isn't length-normalised favours long paragraphs, which is what BM25-style normalisation corrects. Before treating it as a mechanism I need to check whether the score actually rises with length among distractors, and where length lives in the network. I'll also check what the three non-question last-layer heads (0, 6, 11) read.\n\n```python\n# Hypothesis: the score rises with paragraph length among distractors (un-normalised lexical evidence); L5 heads 0/6/11 read the paragraph rather than the question.\nnt = F.ntok.values.astype(float); lnt = cq(np.log(nt))\nprint(\"within-q corr(score, log ntok): distractors %.2f | golds %.2f | all %.2f\" % (np.corrcoef(s0c[dist], lnt[dist])[0,1], np.corrcoef(s0c[gold], lnt[gold])[0,1], np.corrcoef(s0c, lnt)[0,1]))\nprint(\"within-q corr(overlap, log ntok) distractors %.2f; corr(score, log ntok | overlap-partialled, distr) %.2f\" % (np.corrcoef(ovc[dist], lnt[dist])[0,1],\n      np.corrcoef(s0c[dist] - np.polyval(np.polyfit(ovc[dist], s0c[dist], 1), ovc[dist]), lnt[dist])[0,1]))\nprint(\"gold vs distractor log-ntok gap (within-q, A/B): %.2f / %.2f\" % (lnt[A & gold].mean() - lnt[A & dist].mean(), lnt[~A & gold].mean() - lnt[~A & dist].mean()))\n# where is length at CLS? per layer corr of best single coordinate and of length-cov direction (A) tested on B\nfor L in range(1, 7):\n    X = cq(C[:, L]); dl = (X[A] * lnt[A, None]).mean(0); dl /= np.linalg.norm(dl); p = X @ dl\n    print(\"L%d length-dir: corr(log ntok) B=%.2f | corr(score) B=%.2f | cos(m6)=%s\" % (L, np.corrcoef(p[~A], lnt[~A])[0,1], np.corrcoef(p[~A], s0c[~A])[0,1], (\"%.2f\" % (dl @ m6)) if L == 6 else \"-\"))\n# what do L5 heads 0, 6, 11 and L4 heads attend to from CLS? (q0, 8 inputs) mass on CLS itself / [SEP]s / body\nfor L, hs in [(5, [0, 6, 11]), (4, [1, 4, 10, 11])]:\n    for h in hs:\n        cls_m = np.mean([att[k, L, h, 0, 0] for k in range(8)]); sep_m = np.mean([att[k, L, h, 0, sep_pos[k]].sum() for k in range(8)])\n        print(\"L%d h%d: CLS->self %.2f  ->[SEP]s %.2f  ->question %.2f  ->title %.2f  ->body %.2f\" % (L, h, cls_m, sep_m, res[L, h, 0], res[L, h, 1], 1 - cls_m - sep_m - res[L, h, 0] - res[L, h, 1]))\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4145,"cache_read_input_tokens":58152,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4145},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aabc22c4-c493-4939-8aac-1a1cdf219a55","uuid":"305e9ddf-b563-4ce0-82cd-27a7c63b1cff","timestamp":"2026-11-13T14:15:49.937Z","request_id":"req_011Ch14t88TMA2RMWNmpqxrh"}
